On Wed, 2019-11-27 at 14:20 +0000, Ignacio Jaureguiberry wrote:
> Finally I'm running wpa_supplicant with this wpa.conf config file:
> =

> ```
> # Configure OpenSSL to load the PKCS#11 engine and tpm2-pkcs11 module
> pkcs11_engine_path=3D/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/engines-1.1/pkcs11.so
> pkcs11_module_path=3D/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/pkcs11/libtpm2_pkcs11.so
> =

> network=3D{
>         ssid=3D"TPMAP"
>         key_mgmt=3DWPA-EAP
>         eap=3DTLS
>         identity=3D"testing"
> =

>         # use OpenSSL PKCS#11 engine for this network
>         engine=3D1
>         engine_id=3D"pkcs11"
> =

>         pin=3D"myuserpin"
> =

>         # select the private key on ID (output from p11tool above)
>         key_id=3D"62356461333863313935373361313763"
> =

>         # CA certificate and client signed certificate paths
>         ca_cert=3D"/root/wpa_supplicant/ca.pem"
>         client_cert=3D"/root/wpa_supplicant/client_tpm.pem"
> }
> ```

Most of that should be unnecessary on a correctly configured system.
Just use the PKCS#11 URI for the key as your private_key field.

     private_key=3D"pkcs11:manufacturer=3Dpiv_II;id=3D%01"

When it sees that, wpa_supplicant will automatically infer that it
needs to use the "pkcs11" engine, and load it by name. So as long as
it's installed correctly where OpenSSL will find it, that'll be done.
And the pkcs11 engine uses p11-kit-proxy.so by default, so any PKCS#11
modules which are installed correctly with a p11-kit module file to
describe them will be available to it.


In your case though, maybe you'd be better off using one of the TPM
engines instead of doing it through PKCS#11?



